### What is the Overlapping Intervals problem?

The problem gives a set of intervals and asks a simple yes/no question: do any two overlap? This is the lightweight existence-check variant of the merge-intervals family. The naive approach compares every pair (O(n squared)), but sorting reduces it to a single linear scan.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class OverlappingIntervals {
    public boolean hasOverlap(int[][] intervals) {
        if (intervals.length <= 1) return false;
        Arrays.sort(intervals, Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0]));

        for (int i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++) {
            if (intervals[i][0] < intervals[i - 1][1]) {
                return true; // overlap found
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] bookings = {{9, 12}, {13, 15}, {11, 14}};
        System.out.println("Has overlap: " + new OverlappingIntervals().hasOverlap(bookings));
    }
}
```

```text
Has overlap: true
```

### Why does sorting collapse all-pairs to adjacency?

After sorting by start, any overlap must involve adjacent intervals in the sorted order. If interval A comes before interval B in start order and they overlap, then B starts before A ends, which surfaces when we compare B to its immediate predecessor A. No non-adjacent pair can overlap without the adjacency check also detecting it.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WhyAdjacency {
    // Sorted: [1,5], [3,7], [10,12]
    // [3,7] starts before [1,5] ends (3 < 5) => overlap
    // [10,12] starts after [3,7] ends (10 > 7) => no overlap
    // No need to check [1,5] vs [10,12] because if they overlapped,
    // [3,7] would overlap one of them too (by the sorted start order).

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("After sorting, overlap surfaces between adjacent pairs.");
    }
}
```

### How does the touching policy work?

Whether intervals that touch (one ends exactly where the next begins) count as overlapping depends on the domain. For booking windows where back-to-back use is allowed, use strict less-than. For time ranges where touching means a conflict, use less-than-or-equal.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TouchingPolicyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int prevEnd = 10, curStart = 10;

        // Strict < : touching is NOT overlapping
        System.out.println("< : overlap? " + (curStart < prevEnd)); // false

        // <= : touching IS overlapping
        System.out.println("<= : overlap? " + (curStart <= prevEnd)); // true
    }
}
```

```text
< : overlap? false
<= : overlap? true
```

### How is the banking mini-project structured?

At National Bank of India, the settlement scheduler validates that no two booking windows for the same clearing channel overlap. The validator sorts bookings by start and checks adjacent pairs.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class SettlementBookingValidator {
    record Booking(int startHour, int endHour, String desk) {}

    public boolean hasConflict(List<Booking> bookings) {
        bookings.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(Booking::startHour));
        for (int i = 1; i < bookings.size(); i++) {
            if (bookings.get(i).startHour() < bookings.get(i - 1).endHour()) {
                System.out.println("Conflict: " + bookings.get(i - 1).desk()
                    + " and " + bookings.get(i).desk());
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Booking> bookings = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            new Booking(9, 12, "DeskA"),
            new Booking(11, 14, "DeskB"),
            new Booking(15, 17, "DeskC")
        ));
        boolean conflict = new SettlementBookingValidator().hasConflict(bookings);
        System.out.println("Has conflict: " + conflict);
    }
}
```

```text
Conflict: DeskA and DeskB
Has conflict: true
```

### How is this different from Merge Intervals?

| Feature | Detect Overlap | Merge Intervals |
|---|---|---|
| Goal | boolean: any overlap? | list of merged intervals |
| Early exit | yes, on first overlap | no, must process all |
| Output | true or false | merged interval list |
| Complexity | same O(n log n) sort | same, but full sweep |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class DetectVsMerge {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Detect: boolean, early exit on first overlap.");
        System.out.println("Merge: full sweep, produces merged list.");
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single interval (no possible overlap), all intervals identical (overlap), no overlapping intervals, and intervals fully contained within each other.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class OverlapEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        OverlappingIntervals solver = new OverlappingIntervals();

        System.out.println("Single: " + solver.hasOverlap(new int[][]{{1,5}}));
        System.out.println("Identical: " + solver.hasOverlap(new int[][]{{1,5},{1,5}}));
        System.out.println("No overlap: " + solver.hasOverlap(new int[][]{{1,3},{5,7},{9,11}}));
        System.out.println("Contained: " + solver.hasOverlap(new int[][]{{1,10},{3,5}}));
    }
}
```

```text
Single: false
Identical: true
No overlap: false
Contained: true
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is comparing with the wrong end value. After sorting by start, the previous interval might have a smaller end than the one before it (if a contained interval was sorted between them). For a pure existence check, comparing to the immediate predecessor's end is sufficient because we return on the first clash. For detecting all overlaps, you would need to track the running maximum end.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: not sorted, comparing arbitrary pairs
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
        if (overlap(i, j)) return true; // O(n^2)
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: sort + adjacent check, O(n log n)
Arrays.sort(intervals, Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0]));
for (int i = 1; i < intervals.length; i++)
    if (intervals[i][0] < intervals[i - 1][1]) return true;
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer recognises this as the lightweight warm-up of the merge-intervals family. They know that the sort collapses an all-pairs question into a single adjacency scan, and that the touching policy (strict vs non-strict) is a real domain decision. They also know that the containment case is handled for free: a contained interval starts before its enclosing interval ends.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorOverlapNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Lightweight warm-up of merge-intervals family.");
        System.out.println("Sort collapses all-pairs O(n^2) to adjacency O(n).");
        System.out.println("Touching policy is a domain decision, not a coding detail.");
        System.out.println("Containment handled for free by the start < prevEnd check.");
    }
}
```
